      "tile_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift",
      "title": "A/B Test Structure and Lift",
      "summary": "Introduces the structure of a randomised A/B test (control group, treatment group, metric) and the concept of lift (relative improvement) using QuickRide's checkout-button experiment. Learners identify the parts of an experiment and compute lift from control and treatment conversion rates.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s01",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stats.ab_test_structure"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "A/B Test Structure",
          "title": "Two groups, one metric, one baseline",
          "lede": "QuickRide's product team wants to know whether a redesigned checkout button gets more users to complete an order. Rather than guess, they run a controlled test \u2014 showing each user one version at random and measuring what happens.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s01.b1",
              "text": "Randomised experiments: control vs treatment",
              "level": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s01.b2",
              "role": "definition",
              "text": "Studies where researchers assign treatments to cases are called **experiments**. When the assignment includes randomisation, it is called a **randomised experiment**. Researchers randomly assign cases to a **control group** and a **treatment group** so that differences between the groups that cannot otherwise be controlled are evened out.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#f1",
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#f2"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#tb2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s01.b3",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "**A/B testing** works by showing two variants of a page or experience to users at random: the original (**control, or A**) and a modified version (**variation, or B**). Engagement with each version is measured and then compared against the baseline \u2014 the control. For the checkout button: the existing button is A, the redesigned button is B, and the metric compared is the share of each group that completes an order.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#f3"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#tb3"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
