        {
          "step_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s05",
          "type": "explainer",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stats.lift_interpretation"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Lift",
          "title": "Turning raw rates into a single comparison figure",
          "lede": "The checkout test is done: the control button converted 20 % of users, the redesigned button converted 22 %. Those two numbers need to be turned into a comparison that's easy to talk about \u2014 that's where lift comes in.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s05.b1",
              "text": "Relative improvement (lift)",
              "level": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s05.b2",
              "role": "definition",
              "text": "**Relative improvement (lift)** is the percentage difference in the variation's conversion rate over the baseline (control) conversion rate. For example, a 10 % relative improvement over a 25 % baseline means the variation's conversion rate is 27.5 %. Relative improvement describes the variation's change **as a percentage of the baseline's rate** \u2014 not as a percentage-point difference.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#f1",
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#f2"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#tb2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s05.b3",
              "role": "example",
              "text": "Applying that to the checkout test: treatment rate 22 % minus control rate 20 % is a **2-percentage-point** absolute difference. As a percentage of the 20 % baseline: 2 \u00f7 20 \u00d7 100 = **10 % lift**. The redesigned button produced a 10 % relative improvement over the original.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#f1"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#tb3"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "side_by_side",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s05.b4",
              "left_label": "Control (A) \u2014 original button",
              "right_label": "Treatment (B) \u2014 redesigned button",
              "left": {
                "type": "data_table",
                "result_of": {
                  "runtime": "sql",
                  "sql": "SELECT \"group\", users, conversions, conversion_rate FROM ab_test_summary WHERE \"group\" = 'control'",
                  "asset_mounts": [
                    {
                      "asset_id": "ds_ab_test_summary",
                      "mount_path": "ab_test_summary"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "caption": "Control group: 500 users, 100 conversions, rate = 0.20"
              },
              "right": {
                "type": "data_table",
                "result_of": {
                  "runtime": "sql",
                  "sql": "SELECT \"group\", users, conversions, conversion_rate FROM ab_test_summary WHERE \"group\" = 'treatment'",
                  "asset_mounts": [
                    {
                      "asset_id": "ds_ab_test_summary",
                      "mount_path": "ab_test_summary"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                "caption": "Treatment group: 500 users, 110 conversions, rate = 0.22"
              },
              "takeaway": "Absolute difference: 0.22 \u2212 0.20 = 0.02 (2 pp). Lift: 0.02 \u00f7 0.20 \u00d7 100 = 10 % \u2014 the redesigned button produced a 10 % relative improvement over the baseline.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#f1",
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#f2"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#tb2",
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#tb3"
                ],
                "anchors": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#visual"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.s05.b5",
              "role": "takeaway",
              "text": "Analyzing an A/B test's results means determining whether the variation had a **positive**, **negative**, or **neutral** effect compared with the baseline (control). A **positive lift** means the variation's rate came out above the control's in this test. A **negative lift** means it came out below. A **neutral** result means the rates were effectively the same.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#f3"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.lift_interpretation#tb4"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
