      "remediation_steps": [
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.r01",
          "type": "remediation",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stats.ab_test_structure"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's review",
          "title": "What makes an experiment randomised?",
          "lede": "The check asked you to distinguish a randomised experiment from a non-randomised study. Here's the key contrast.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.r01.b1",
              "role": "definition",
              "text": "Studies where researchers **assign** treatments to cases are called **experiments**. When that assignment includes **randomisation**, it is a **randomised experiment**. Randomly assigning cases to a control group and a treatment group evens out differences between the groups that cannot otherwise be controlled \u2014 that's what makes the comparison fair.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#f1",
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#f2"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#tb2"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.r01.b2",
              "role": "misconception",
              "text": "A non-randomised study lets participants choose their own group, or assigns them by convenience. Without random assignment, the two groups may differ in ways that affect the outcome \u2014 so you can't be sure the treatment caused any difference you observe. In the checkout-button test, users are randomly shown one version or the other, which is what makes it a randomised experiment.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#f1",
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#f2"
                ],
                "teaching_block_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#tb2",
                  "stats.ab_test_structure#tb3"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "stats.ab_test_structure"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "stats.ab_test_structure#f1",
              "stats.ab_test_structure#f2"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "stats.ab_test_structure#tb2",
              "stats.ab_test_structure#tb3"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "step_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.r02",
          "type": "remediation",
          "concept_ids": [
            "stats.ab_test_structure"
          ],
          "eyebrow": "Let's review",
          "title": "Identifying the groups and metric in an A/B test",
          "lede": "The hands-on asked you to identify the control group, treatment group, and the metric being compared. Here's how to read the table.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "prose",
              "block_id": "tile_stats_ab_test_lift.r02.b1",
              "role": "mechanism",
              "text": "In the user records table, the **`group`** column tells you which arm each user was assigned to: `'control'` for the original button (A) and `'treatment'` for the redesigned button (B). The **`converted`** column records the metric: 1 if the user completed an order, 0 if not. The metric being compared between the two groups is the **share of users who converted** \u2014 the conversion rate.",
              "lineage": {
                "concept_ids": [
                  "stats.ab_test_structure"
                ],
                "fact_ids": [
